    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "During the first year of life, infants’ cortex undergoes development that is essential for typical brain sensory function in adult years. It is during this early infancy period of life that essential sensorimotor capabilities occur, such as the onset of color vision, color contrast, spatial sensitivity, grasping behaviors and rolling over. Despite knowledge of its significance, understanding of this critical period is still limited regarding the underlying mechanisms, rate and order of development. Leveraging MRI technology and utilizing the quantity measurement Longitudinal Relaxation Rate (R1), which is directly proportional to microstructural tissue density, gives a noninvasive way of comparing longitudinally the density of infant cortical tissue across the first year of life.  This thesis project seeks to gain better understanding of how microstructural tissue develops in first year of infancy.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Allen, A. (2024). Now You See Me: Cortical Microstructural Tissue Development in Visual System During First Year of Infant Life. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/rs394bs0359.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
